# 29. Random values

This chapter is about the module `random`: samples, shuffles, choices,
random numbers and UUIDs. It covers the two forms each draw has, the seeded
one and the unseeded one, and the evaluation seed that makes a program's
random output repeatable.

## The module

`random` is not in the prelude: a program writes `import random`. Each draw
has two forms. With a `seed` argument it is **pure**: its result is a
function of the seed, its input and its call site, the same on every run.
Without one it is **effectful** and draws from the **evaluation seed**, fixed
once per evaluation.

The generator is ChaCha12. A seeded draw is predictable by design, so no draw
of `random` is fit for a key, a token or any other secret.

## Samples, shuffles and choices

`sample(n)` is `n` elements drawn without replacement, in the order they have
in the input:

```hql
import random
range(1, 21) | sample(5, seed = 7)
```

```text
[1, 5, 9, 13, 16]
```

`shuffle` is every element in a uniformly random order, and `choice` one
element drawn uniformly:

```hql
import random
titles = cards | filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book") | map(c => c.title)
{shuffled: titles | shuffle(seed = 7), picked: titles | choice(seed = 7)}
```

```text
{picked: "A Room of One's Own", shuffled: ["The Hobbit", "Frankenstein", "The Common Reader", "Mrs Dalloway", "Dune", "A Room of One's Own", "Leaves of Grass"]}
```

`choice` is an `Option`: over an empty input it is `none`. `sample(n)` with
`n` at or above the input's size is the whole input, and a negative `n` is an
evaluation error:

```hql
import random
nothing = [1, 2, 3] | filter(n => n > 5)
[nothing | choice(seed = 1), [1, 2, 3] | choice(seed = 1)]
```

```text
[none, 1]
```

A collection without an order, such as a `Set`, is sorted by its elements
before it is sampled, so the sample does not depend on how the set was built.

## A seed

A seeded draw is a function of its call site, its position in the program
text, as well as of its seed. Two calls with one seed at two places are two
draws:

```hql
import random
deck = ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e"]
[deck | shuffle(seed = 1), deck | shuffle(seed = 1)]
```

```text
[["e", "c", "b", "d", "a"], ["a", "c", "e", "b", "d"]]
```

One call site with one seed gives one result each time it is reached. A
seeded draw inside a `map` repeats; a seed that varies with the element
varies the draw:

```hql
import random
deck = ["a", "b", "c", "d"]
{same: range(0, 3) | map(_ => deck | shuffle(seed = 1)), varied: range(0, 3) | map(i => deck | shuffle(seed = i))}
```

```text
{same: [["c", "b", "d", "a"], ["c", "b", "d", "a"], ["c", "b", "d", "a"]], varied: [["d", "c", "b", "a"], ["c", "a", "d", "b"], ["d", "c", "a", "b"]]}
```

Moving a seeded call to another place in the program text changes its
result; running the same program again does not.

## The evaluation seed

An unseeded draw reads the evaluation seed. `--seed <int>` fixes it; without
`--seed`, the seed comes from the host's entropy. Each unseeded call site
draws from a stream derived from the evaluation seed and its position in the
program, so two call sites draw independently, and a program run twice with
one `--seed` writes the same values:

<!-- run: --seed 7 -->
```hql
import random
xs = range(1, 11)
{sample: xs | sample(3), shuffled: xs | shuffle, picked: xs | choice}
```

```text
{picked: 9, sample: [1, 2, 4], shuffled: [10, 2, 1, 4, 9, 5, 6, 8, 7, 3]}
```

An evaluation that takes its seed from the host reports it at `info`, with
the `--seed` that repeats it:

```text
$ hql --report-level info eval 'import random
range(1, 11) | shuffle | size'
10
execute info[effect]: the evaluation seed is -7270767200120302920; `--seed -7270767200120302920` repeats this evaluation
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | import random
  | ^
```

Without `--seed`, a block's values differ from run to run, but what holds of
every draw holds of each: a sample of `n` has `n` elements, and a shuffle
holds every element once:

```hql
import random
[range(0, 1000) | sample(3) | size, (range(0, 29) | shuffle | sort) == range(0, 29)]
```

```text
[3, true]
```

## Numbers

`random_int(low, high)` is an `Int` drawn uniformly from `low` to `high`,
both included, and `random_float()` a `Float` from `0.0` included to `1.0`
excluded. Both are effectful and have no seeded form, so `--seed` is what
makes them repeatable:

<!-- run: --seed 7 -->
```hql
import random
{dice: range(0, 5) | map(_ => random_int(1, 6)), fraction: random_float()}
```

```text
{dice: [4, 6, 4, 1, 6], fraction: 0.5037399186078297}
```

`high` below `low` is an evaluation error:

<!-- run: --seed 7 -->
```hql
import random
random_int(6, 1)
```

```text
execute failure[invalid-argument]: `random_int` needs `high` at or above `low`, and 1 is below 6
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | random_int(6, 1)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

## UUIDs

A UUID is written as lowercase hyphenated text. `uuid(name, namespace)` is
the version 5 UUID of a name in a namespace: a function of the two, with no
draw. The namespace is a UUID's text, or one of the standard namespaces
`"dns"`, `"url"`, `"oid"` and `"x500"`:

```hql
import random
uuid("www.example.com", "dns")
```

```text
"2ed6657d-e927-568b-95e1-2665a8aea6a2"
```

`uuid()` is a version 7 UUID. Its first 48 bits are the evaluation instant's
Unix milliseconds and the next 42 a counter, so the UUIDs of one evaluation
are distinct and sort in the order they were drawn:

```hql
import random
ids = range(0, 20) | map(_ => uuid())
[ids == sort(ids), size(Set(ids)) == 20]
```

```text
[true, true]
```

Its remaining bits come from the evaluation seed. `--now` fixes the
evaluation instant ([[02-data-types/time#the-evaluation-clock|the evaluation clock]])
and `--seed` the seed, and the two together make `uuid()` repeatable:

<!-- run: --seed 7 --now 2026-10-24T07:30:00Z -->
```hql
import random
[uuid(), uuid()]
```

```text
["01a16cd1-04c0-706c-9aa2-de28777558c7", "01a16cd1-04c0-706c-9aa2-de29b51d9000"]
```

## A stream

`sample(n)` also takes a stream, by reservoir sampling: the step holds `n`
elements, never the stream, and gives its sample when the stream ends. A
stream that does not end gives no sample. The stream form is effectful
only.

